[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m **Perturbation 1: Perturb(A)**  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m - Perturb(A) means we set A to 0.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m - According to the semantics, a node is 1 if it is a downstream descendant of the perturbed node (in the graph).  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m - So A is perturbed to 0.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m - The downstream descendants of A are C and D (since A -> C and A -> D).  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m - Therefore:  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m   - A: 0 (perturbed),  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m   - B: 0 (not a descendant of A),  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m   - C: 1 (descendant of A),  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m   - D: 1 (descendant of A).  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m - So the output should be: A:0, B:0, C:1, D:1.  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3563078)[0m - This matches the given observation: `Perturb(A) -> A:0 B:0 C:1 D:1`. ✅
